                "text": "Analyse the examples and solve the task.\n\nThe task involves a grid containing vertical and horizontal lines of colors intersecting on a black (0) background.\nLooking at the compression:\nIn Example 1:\nInput size: 10 rows, 8 cols.\nVertical lines at cols: 2 (color 4), 4 (color 8), 6 (color 2).\nHorizontal lines at rows: 3 (color 3), 8 (color 6).\nOutput size: 5 rows, 7 cols.\nOutput rows:\nrow 0: empty\nrow 1: line 3\nrow 2: empty\nrow 3: line 6\nrow 4: empty\nNotice that consecutive empty regions (rows/cols of 0s) are compressed to a single empty row/column!\nWait, let's check cols in Example 1:\nCols in input:\ncols 0,1: empty -> 1 empty col (col 0)\ncol 2: vertical line (4) -> col 1\ncol 3: empty -> 1 empty col (col 2)\ncol 4: vertical line (8) -> col 3\ncol 5: empty -> 1 empty col (col 4)\ncol 6: vertical line (2) -> col 5\ncol 7: empty -> 1 empty col (col 6)\nTotal cols: 7! That matches!\nAnd rows in Example 1:\nrows 0,1,2: empty -> row 0\nrow 3: line 3 -> row 1\nrows 4,5,6,7: empty -> row 2\nrow 8: line 6 -> row 3\nrow 9: empty -> row 4\nTotal rows: 5! That matches!\n\nLet's check Example 2:\nInput: 12 rows, 13 cols.\nVertical lines:\ncols 4 (3), 8 (2).\nLeft of 4: cols 0..3 (empty) -> 1 col\ncol 4 (3) -> 1 col\nbetween 4 and 8: cols 5..7 (empty) -> 1 col\ncol 8 (2) -> 1 col\nright of 8: cols 9..12 (empty) -> 1 col\nTotal output cols: 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 5 cols.\nRows in Example 2:\nrow 0: empty -> 1 row\nrow 1: line 5 -> 1 row\nrow 2: empty -> 1 row\nrow 3: line 9 -> 1 row\nrows 4,5: empty -> 1 row\nrow 6: line 4 -> 1 row\nrows 7,8: empty -> 1 row\nrow 9: line 7 -> 1 row\nrows 10,11: empty -> 1 row\nTotal output rows: 9 rows!\nMatches output of Example 2 (9x5)!\n\nSo any contiguous run of background (0) rows/columns is compressed to exactly 1 row/column.\nLines (rows/columns containing non-zero line colors) are kept as 1 row/column.\nAt intersections, the input already shows which line is on top (or both lines keep their intersection value, which is just taken directly from the grid at those coordinates).\nWait, if we just select:\n- for each contiguous block of empty rows, pick one representative empty row.\n- for each line row, pick that row.\n- for each contiguous block of empty cols, pick one representative empty col.\n- for each line col, pick that col.\nAnd take the subgrid formed by these selected row and column indices!\n\nLet's verify:\nIn Example 1:\nIntersections:\nLine 3 with 4, 8, 2:\nIn input:\nrow 3: [3,3,4,3,8,3,2,3]\nSelected cols: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7] -> input[3][[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]] = [3, 4, 3, 8, 3, 2, 3].\nOutput row 1: [3, 4, 3, 8, 3, 2, 3]. Exactly matches!\nRow 8: all 6s:\nSelected cols: [6, 6, 6, 6, 6, 6, 6]. Matches!\n\nThis is run-length compression of empty rows and columns into single rows and columns!\nWait, what defines a \"line row\" vs an \"empty row\"?\nAn empty row has non-zero values ONLY at the vertical line positions (and background 0 elsewhere).\nA line row has a horizontal line extending across (or mostly across, except at intersections).\nWait, in Example 1, row 0 has: [0,0,4,0,8,0,2,0] - only vertical line values, no horizontal line.\nSo an empty row is a row that does not contain a horizontal line.\nSimilarly, an empty column is a column that does not contain a vertical line.\n\nLet's test this logic! Everything is crystal clear.Please provide the first held-out test input.",
